<section><h2>The upload sequence</h2><ol class="docs-steps"><li><strong>Resolve identity.</strong> Read the catalog for opaque project and folder IDs. Check the credential’s project scope and permitted actions.</li><li><strong>Prepare the file.</strong> Compute SHA-256, exact MIME type, byte count, dimensions and duration. Prepare a real JPEG preview.</li><li><strong>Begin once, transfer, complete.</strong> Persist an idempotency key with the exact body. Upload to the returned storage URLs, then complete that same session. Signed storage URLs do not receive the Review bearer header.</li><li><strong>Wait for readiness.</strong> Poll the video job until Ready or Failed. Completion of byte transfer alone does not mean derivatives are available.</li><li><strong>Read the pinned take.</strong> Use its version ID for media and creative metadata. Short-lived media URLs are delivery capabilities, not permanent identifiers.</li></ol><aside class="docs-note"><strong>Retry the same operation.</strong><p>After an uncertain begin or completion response, reuse its recorded key and session rather than creating another upload. A changed request body needs a new idempotency key.</p></aside></section>
<section><h2>Choose the right identity</h2><div class="docs-table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Identity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Placement and access</td><td>Project ID and optional real folder ID</td></tr><tr><td>A shot or review item</td><td>Asset ID; production code where assigned</td></tr><tr><td>Exact media, prompt or reference image</td><td>Version ID</td></tr><tr><td>Current production pick</td><td>The asset’s current-version relationship</td></tr><tr><td>Delivery to another app</td><td>Explicit publication grant and immutable source asset/version tuple</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Version metadata uses a revision check to protect concurrent saves. The changes feed may repeat items near its cursor, so upsert by ID. It reports specific merged/misfiled removals and is not a general deletion feed. See the agent guide for exact semantics.</p></section>
<section><h2>Follow the responsibilities</h2><div class="docs-pair"><div><h3>Catalog and decisions</h3><p>Convex owns project/folder records, asset and version identities, review state, creative metadata and delivery consent.</p></div><div><h3>Media and preparation</h3><p>RustFS holds original and derivative bytes. Trigger.dev coordinates video jobs; FFmpeg prepares thumbnails and scrub sprites. Review Room authorizes delivery.</p></div></div><a class="docs-resource" href={resolve('/docs/[diagram]',{diagram:'media-flow.html'})}><strong>Explore the media flow</strong><span>Upload, verification, video processing, review and explicit publishing, with an optional step-through sequence.</span></a><a class="docs-resource" href={resolve('/docs/[diagram]',{diagram:'metadata-model.html'})}><strong>Explore metadata relationships</strong><span>Projects, folders, assets, versions, feedback and publication access. Comments are asset-level; creative metadata is version-level.</span></a></section>
<section><h2>Stay inside the granted scope</h2><p>Use a project-scoped credential for a series integration. Upload permission is not publication permission. Locked folders belong to the credential that created them; that ownership is not interchangeable with an owner browser session. Never log bearer keys or signed media URLs.</p><p>Implementation and deployment details belong in <a href={resolve('/docs/operations')}>owner operations</a>. General collaborators do not need infrastructure access to understand the review workflow.</p></section>
